WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.160
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.700
As animation movies and computer game graphics
become more and more realistic, they draw

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.080
us more and more into their own worlds.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:17.200
However, when we see cars, mugs, minerals
and paintings, and similar materials, we often

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.640
feel that something is not right there, and
the illusion quickly crumbles.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:27.060
This is such a peculiar collection of materials...so
what is the common denominator between them?

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.810
Normally, to create these beautiful images,
we use programs that create millions and millions

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:36.590
of rays of light and simulate how they bounce
off of the objects within the scene.

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:41.140
However, most of these programs bounce these
rays off of the surface of these objects,

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:46.450
where in reality, there are many sophisticated
multi-layered materials with all kinds of

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:48.450
coatings and varnishes.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:53.050
Such a simple surface model is not adequate
to model these multiple layers.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:57.670
This new technique is able to simulate not
only these surface interactions, but how light

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:03.210
is scattered, transferred and absorbed within
these layers, enabling us to create even more

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:06.320
beautiful images with more sophisticated materials.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:11.170
We can envision new material models with any
number of layers and it will be able to handle

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:12.170
it.

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:14.200
However, I left the best part for last.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:18.930
What is even cooler is that it takes advantage
of the regularity of the data and builds a

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:24.700
statistical model that approximates what typically
happens with our light rays within these layers.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:29.090
What this results in is a real-time technique
that still remains accurate.

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:30.280
This is not normal.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.130
This used to take hours!

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:33.520
This is insanity!

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.160
And the whole paper was written by only one
author, Laurent Belcour and was accepted to

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.200
the most prestigious research venue in computer
graphics.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.230
So huge congrats to Laurent for accomplishing
this.

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:48.260
If you would like to learn more about light
transport, I am holding an Master-level course

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.850
on it at the Technical University of Vienna.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:55.520
This course used to take place behind closed
doors, but I feel that the teachings shouldn't

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:01.010
only be available for the 20-30 people who
can afford a University education, but they

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:02.700
should be available for everyone.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:07.590
So, we recorded the entirety of the course
and it is now available for everyone, free

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:08.590
of charge.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:11.590
If you are interested, have a look at the
video description to watch them.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:15.010
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

